
  <p>
    Event-driven architecture is sometimes sold as a messaging layer added at the
    end of the system. In my experience it is the opposite: events are a way of
    distributing responsibilities. Used that way, the system gains autonomy; used
    as a generic bus, it only gains ceremony.
  </p>

  <h2>An event is not a message</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>command</strong> asks for something to happen and expects a specific
    recipient. An <strong>event</strong> states that something already happened and
    does not care who listens. The difference sounds semantic until the system has
    to evolve: commands couple sender and receiver; events let you add consumers
    without touching the producer.
  </p>
  <p>
    In an industrial platform this becomes very concrete: the core decides that a
    task must run and publishes the fact. It does not need to know whether the
    thing that moves it is a forklift, a PLC or a person following an LED.
  </p>

  <h2>What an event carries</h2>
  <p>
    A useful event answers four questions without forcing the consumer to query the
    producer: what happened, to what, when and with which identity. That usually
    means an event identifier, the affected aggregate, a timestamp and the minimum
    payload needed to react.
  </p>
  <p>
    The temptation is to put the complete system state into every event. It is
    convenient at first and expensive later: every consumer couples to a huge
    contract that can no longer change. I prefer small events with a stable
    identifier and an explicit query when more context is needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Boundaries matter more than the broker</h2>
  <p>
    Choosing Kafka, RabbitMQ or your own bus does not fix a badly bounded system.
    If two services share the same data model, the bus only makes the coupling
    asynchronous.
  </p>
  <p>
    The real work is deciding which facts belong to which domain and who is the
    source of truth for each one. In Nakire TPS, each device family had its own
    service and adapters; the core coordinated them but did not know protocols.
    That division gave every event a clear owner.
  </p>

  <h2>Consistency: accepting delay</h2>
  <p>
    When an event takes time to arrive, the system is eventually consistent, not
    instantly consistent. That phrase sounds good in a presentation and demands
    very concrete decisions in production: what the interface shows while state
    converges, which operations can be repeated and how to reconcile what the user
    believes happened with what the system recorded.
  </p>
  <p>
    The solution is not to hide the asynchrony. It is to make it visible:
    intermediate states, timestamps, correlation identifiers and a way to read the
    real state again.
  </p>

  <h2>Idempotency and order</h2>
  <p>
    The same event can arrive twice. Global order is not guaranteed and, even if it
    were, it is not always the order the business needs. Consumers therefore have
    to be idempotent by design, not by luck.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Identify each operation with a stable key.</li>
    <li>Record what has already been applied before applying it again.</li>
    <li>Design state transitions that tolerate repetition.</li>
    <li>Do not depend on order between different aggregates.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In an industrial task, repeating “move the pallet to position 4” cannot mean
    moving it twice or losing the second intention if the first one was already
    completed. That is the kind of detail that decides whether an event-based
    system survives real operations.
  </p>

  <h2>Isolating failure</h2>
  <p>
    A concrete advantage of asynchronous communication is that a consumer that
    stops responding does not block the producer. But the system must decide what
    to do with whatever depended on it: retry, mark the task as unavailable or
    escalate to another device family.
  </p>
  <p>
    That policy is not improvised. It should be written next to the event contract,
    because it is part of the system's observable behaviour.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use events</h2>
  <p>
    If two operations must be atomic or the consumer needs an immediate response to
    continue, a direct call is usually more honest. Adding a bus to a flow that
    always happens in the same order with the same recipient only introduces
    operational complexity.
  </p>
  <p>
    My rule is simple: events when several consumers can react to the same fact,
    when the sender should not know the receivers, or when the work can wait.
    Direct calls when the response is part of the result.
  </p>

  <h2>Closing</h2>
  <p>
    An event architecture is well placed when adding a capability does not touch
    the core, a failed service does not drag the others down and every fact has an
    owner. If the only thing visible in the design is the bus, the boundary work is
    missing: the bus carries facts, it does not define them.
  </p>
